  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nRest of Today-Tuesday...A glance at a loop of GOES-East visible \nsatellite imagery this afternoon shows an upper level low locked \nover the Florida peninsula, evident in the cloud motions. Deep \nmoisture, with PWATs over 2\" and upper level support have allowed \nfor isolated to scattered showers and a few storms to develop prior \nto 2 PM. Expect coverage to increase over the next few hours along \nthe sea breeze, mainly south of Cape Canaveral. Prevailing onshore \nflow will progress this activity inland and to the west coast later \nthis afternoon and into this evening. PoPs decrease accordingly \ntowards sunset. Until then, a few strong storms cannot be ruled out, \ncapable of wind gusts to 50 mph, lightning strikes, and locally \nheavy rainfall of 1-3\". A few showers and storms will be possible \nagain overnight along the coast, as a few cells drift inland along \ncontinued onshore flow. \n\nSimilar conditions are forecast through early this week. The upper \nlevel low persists over the Florida peninsula on Monday, before \nmoving NE and merging with an approaching upper level trough on \nTuesday. Deep moisture (PWATs 2-2.2\") will support scattered to \nnumerous (50-70%) showers and storms in the afternoons. Activity \nlooks to develop along the sea breeze by early afternoon, then drift \ninland along the dominant east coast sea breeze into the evening \nhours. Isolated strong storms will remain possible, though 500 mb \ntemperatures look to warm from near -8 C today to -6 C Tuesday. Late \nnight/early morning showers and storms may continue to drift into \ncoastal areas, as scattered to numerous convection persists over the \nAtlantic waters overnight. Highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s and \nhigh humidity will support peak afternoon heat indices in the upper \n90s to near 100 degrees.\n\nWednesday-Sunday...An upper level trough drops SE out of the Great \nLakes region mid-week, moving offshore. Meanwhile, a surface low \npressure system off of the US east coast drifts NE over the western \nAtlantic. These features are forecast to stack and deepen off of the \nMid-Atlantic coast by Thursday and Friday, then meander offshore of \nNew England into the weekend. Locally, a surface cool front attached \nto this low looks to drop southward into the Florida peninsula \nWednesday. The front is forecast to weaken, slowing as it approaches \nthe local area, where it lingers through the end of the work week \nand finally moves south of the forecast area by the weekend.\n\nLittle change to the sensible impacts Wednesday and Thursday \ncompared to earlier in the week. Deep moisture (PWATs 2-2.3\") \npersists over the area, while the presence of the front maintains \nenough lift for numerous (60-70%) showers and storms, especially in \nthe afternoons. Will also see chances for isolated to scattered \nshowers and storms (20-30%) persist along the coast during the \novernight hours, as NE flow continues. Winds remain around 10 mph or \nless Wednesday, before a tightening pressure gradient behind the \nfront increases winds to 10-15 mph (gusts 20-25 mph) from Melbourne \nnorthward through the day Thursday. Gusts near 30 mph are possible \nalong the Volusia County coastline by the evening hours Thursday, \nwith sustained winds 25-30 mph just offshore. High temperatures in \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s continue.\n\nAs the front moves into south Florida Friday and into the weekend, \ndrier air (PWATs below 1.5\") will begin to filter into northern \nportions of the forecast area. Thus, PoPs diminish from north to \nsouth, becoming isolated to scattered (20-50%). Breezy NE flow \ncontinues, especially on Friday, with winds 10-15+ mph and gusts 20-\n25 mph in the afternoons. Drier air and onshore flow will allow for \nhigh temperatures to drop a few degrees to the mid to upper 80s. \nHowever, the main difference will be the drier air leading to lower \nmax heat indices.",
